  "passage": {
    "title": "The Ancient Olympic Games",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "The first recorded Olympic Games took place in 776 BC at Olympia, a religious site in southern Greece, and continued every four years for nearly twelve centuries. At first, there was a single event, a foot race of about 190 metres, and competitors were drawn from nearby towns. Over time, more events were added, including wrestling, boxing, chariot racing and the pentathlon, and athletes began to arrive from across the Greek world."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "The Games were primarily a religious festival honouring Zeus. A huge statue of the god stood in the temple at Olympia, and each Games began with a sacrifice of one hundred oxen. To allow athletes and spectators to travel safely, a sacred truce was announced before every festival, during which wars were suspended. Although the truce was not always respected, the idea that sport might temporarily bring enemies together is often cited as a model."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Competitors were men only; married women were not allowed even to watch, although unmarried girls could attend. Athletes competed naked and were usually from wealthy families, since training required time and money. There were no team events and no silver or bronze medals: the winner received a wreath of olive leaves, and the other competitors were largely forgotten. Yet the rewards back home could be extraordinary, including free meals for life, statues and large sums of money."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Cheating was taken seriously. Athletes had to swear an oath that they had trained for ten months and would compete fairly. Those caught bribing opponents were fined, and the money paid for bronze statues of Zeus, placed along the path to the stadium with the names of the offenders engraved on them. These statues served as a permanent warning to future competitors."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "The Games ended in the late fourth century AD, when the Roman emperor Theodosius, a Christian, banned pagan festivals. Olympia was later damaged by earthquakes and floods, and buried under layers of soil. Archaeologists began to uncover it in the nineteenth century, which inspired a French educator, Pierre de Coubertin, to organise the first modern Olympics in Athens in 1896."
      }
    ]
  },
